{% adNotice /%}

COPD can make everyday jobs, like washing, dressing and tidying up, feel exhausting. The right aids can ease breathlessness, help you clear mucus from your chest and save your energy for the things that matter to you.

This article starts with the help you can get **free**, so you do not pay for something the NHS or your council already provides.

{% callout type="danger" title="If you are very breathless" %}
No aid replaces your inhalers, rescue pack or treatment plan. If you are struggling to breathe, have chest pain, or your lips or skin look blue or grey, **call 999**. If your symptoms are getting worse, follow your COPD self-management plan or call your GP or NHS 111.
{% /callout %}

## Help you can get free

### From your NHS respiratory team

Ask your GP, practice nurse or respiratory team about:

- **pulmonary rehabilitation**, a free exercise and education course that is one of the best treatments for breathlessness. Read more in [COPD treatment](/conditions/respiratory-and-allergy/copd/treatment/)
- **a COPD self-management plan**, so you know what to do when your symptoms change
- **airway clearance devices and breathing techniques**, taught by a respiratory physiotherapist
- **oxygen**, if tests show you need it. Oxygen is only prescribed after an assessment

### From your council

Your local council can arrange a free **needs assessment**, often with an occupational therapist. They can suggest and provide equipment such as shower seats, perching stools and grab rails. Equipment for daily living that costs under £1,000 should be free if the council says you need it. For bigger changes to your home, you may get a [Disabled Facilities Grant](/support/home-and-equipment/disabled-facilities-grant/).

## Aids you might choose to buy

Where we suggest a product, it is clearly marked **Ad**. Please check that anything you buy suits your needs, and ask your respiratory team or an occupational therapist if you are not sure.

### A handheld fan for breathlessness

Asthma and Lung UK and many respiratory teams suggest a **handheld fan**. Hold it about 15cm (6 inches) from the middle of your face and let the cool air blow towards you. It can take up to 10 minutes to feel the benefit. It works alongside your breathing techniques, not instead of them.

A rechargeable fan you can carry in a bag or pocket means you always have one with you.

{% affiliate name="Jsdoin rechargeable handheld fan with 5 speeds" url="https://link.amazon/B08bgSxRa" merchant="Amazon" %}
A small, rechargeable USB fan with 5 speeds and a display that shows the battery level. It folds so you can stand it on a table. It can help you feel less breathless, but it does not treat COPD.
{% /affiliate %}

### Airway clearance devices

If you cough up a lot of mucus, an **airway clearance device** may make it easier to clear. These are often called **OPEP** devices (oscillating positive expiratory pressure). You breathe out through the device, which creates vibrations and pressure that help loosen mucus. Well known examples include the Aerobika, Acapella and Flutter.

**Talk to your respiratory team before using one.** They are not right for everyone, and a physiotherapist can check it suits you, show you how to use it and may be able to provide one on the NHS.

{% affiliate name="Natural lung exerciser and mucus removal device" url="https://link.amazon/B00aSoDxC" merchant="Amazon" %}
A handheld OPEP breathing device with adjustable resistance, designed to help loosen and clear mucus from your airways. Check with your respiratory team that this type of device is suitable for you before using it.
{% /affiliate %}

### Shower seats and bath stools

Standing in a warm, steamy shower can make you breathless. **Sitting down to wash** saves a lot of energy. It also helps to keep the bathroom door open or run the extractor fan, and to use a towelling robe rather than drying yourself with a towel.

Check the size of your shower or bath, and the most weight the seat can hold, before you buy. Your council's occupational therapist can advise you, and may provide a seat free.

{% affiliate name="Drive rotating bath and shower stool with swivel seat" url="https://link.amazon/B00vK3kd5" merchant="Amazon" %}
A round shower stool with a seat that turns, so you can move around to wash without twisting or standing up. Useful in smaller showers.
{% /affiliate %}

{% affiliate name="ZENO height adjustable shower chair with padded armrests" url="https://link.amazon/B0bddPsSK" merchant="Amazon" %}
A shower chair with 6 height settings, padded armrests to help you sit down and stand up, and non-slip feet. It holds up to 135kg.
{% /affiliate %}

{% affiliate name="HOMLAND adjustable height shower chair" url="https://link.amazon/B0aBZaBxI" merchant="Amazon" %}
A lightweight shower stool with an aluminium frame that will not rust, adjustable height and non-slip feet. It holds up to 181kg.
{% /affiliate %}

### Perching stools

A **perching stool** is a tall seat with a sloping top that lets you half sit, half stand. It is useful for jobs you would normally stand for, such as preparing food, washing up, shaving or doing your hair, so you use less energy and get less breathless.

{% affiliate name="NRS height adjustable perching stool with armrests" url="https://link.amazon/B0cFgHFF5" merchant="Amazon" %}
A height adjustable perching stool with armrests and a padded seat, for resting while you work at a kitchen counter, sink or basin. Ask an occupational therapist to check the right height for you.
{% /affiliate %}

### Grabbers and reachers

Bending down and reaching up can make you breathless. A **grabber** (also called a reacher) lets you pick things up from the floor or reach high shelves without stretching or bending.

{% affiliate name="Foldable 91cm grabber stick" url="https://link.amazon/B03tFMFCP" merchant="Amazon" %}
A lightweight aluminium grabber, 91cm long, with a head that turns and a comfortable handle. It folds down for storage.
{% /affiliate %}

### Pulse oximeters

A **pulse oximeter** clips onto your finger and measures the oxygen level in your blood. Some people with COPD are asked to check their levels at home.

- only use one if your GP or respiratory team has asked you to, and told you what your normal reading is and what to do if it drops
- readings can be **less accurate on darker skin**, and with cold hands or nail varnish
- **always go by how you feel**, not just the number. If you feel very unwell, get help even if the reading looks normal

{% affiliate name="AILE pulse oximeter with large display" url="https://link.amazon/B03LDdjh5" merchant="Amazon" %}
A fingertip pulse oximeter with a large, easy to read screen and a lanyard. It shows your oxygen level and pulse. It is not a replacement for medical advice, and readings may be less accurate on darker skin.
{% /affiliate %}

## Other everyday tips

- **Pace yourself.** Spread jobs across the day and week, and rest before you get too tired. Read [living well with COPD](/conditions/respiratory-and-allergy/copd/living-well/)
- **Keep things within reach.** Store things you use often between waist and shoulder height
- **Keep warm.** Cold air can make breathlessness worse. Wear a scarf over your nose and mouth in cold weather
- **Use a trolley** to carry things around the house, rather than carrying them

## Help with costs

- **Prescriptions** are free for everyone in Wales. In England, a **prescription prepayment certificate** can save money if you need several items. Check with our [NHS health costs check](/support/health-costs/checker/).
- If COPD affects your daily life, you may be able to get [PIP](/benefits/pip/), or [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) if you are over State Pension age. Read [COPD, work and benefits](/conditions/respiratory-and-allergy/copd/benefits-and-work/).
- **VAT relief** may apply to equipment designed for disabled people, including some bathing and mobility aids. See [VAT relief on disability equipment](/support/home-and-equipment/vat-relief/).
